{#
  Migration 0107 — APPEND-ONLY, EVERYWHERE IT MATTERS.

  ARCHITECTURE.md §5.11 item 9 · §16 MI01 · spec/invariants/I01-append-only.md ·
  spec/custody/ledger-schema.md §3.

  Four lines, and they are the cheapest invariant in the repository per unit of value.

  WHY A TRIGGER WHEN THE GRANT MATRIX ALREADY WITHHOLDS UPDATE AND DELETE.
  Because the grant matrix is CLUSTER state and a restore into a new cluster does not carry it,
  because an incident is exactly when somebody is granted more than they should have, and because
  the owner role can always grant itself anything. Three mechanisms, deliberately redundant:

      1. revoked privileges          — 42501, the cheapest refusal, and the first to be lost
      2. this trigger                — P0001, travels with the schema, survives a restore
      3. RESTRICTIVE row-level security policies — 42501 again, and independent of both

  Refusal depth of three on the append-only claim, and the unwelding suite measures it by
  disabling each in turn and asserting the write still fails. At runtime this one fires first
  and deterministically, which is why the corpus asserts P0001 and nothing else.

  WHY NO `WHEN` CLAUSE AND NO CONDITION IN THE BODY. There is no legal mutation of these tables.
  A condition would be a place to add an exception, and an exception to append-only is not a
  narrower rule — it is a different rule, with a different name, in a different file. The one
  table that genuinely needs one is `disposition`, and it gets its own function (0104) whose
  whole body is the argument for the exception.

  WHY IT COVERS DELETE AS WELL AS UPDATE. A record deleted and a record rewritten are the same
  event from the archive's point of view, and the Crimes (Document Destruction) Act 2006 (Vic)
  does not distinguish them either. `BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE` is one trigger per table, measured
  working on v26.2.5 including the DELETE arm, where the function raises before any question of a
  return value arises.

  THE FUNCTION IS NOT THE INVARIANT. This file creates a function; the invariant is created by
  the WELDS, migration 0128 and its lettered companions, and by nothing else. A table that owes
  an append-only weld and has not got one is not protected by the existence of this function, and
  0128's header names every table it welds and every table it does not.

  PLATFORM: no reads, so no `(NEW).col` question arises. The body cannot fall out of date with a
  schema change because it does not mention a column.
#}
